==English==

===Etymology===
From {{compound|en|break|point}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-breakpoint.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|programming}} A [[point]] in a [[program]] at which [[operation]] may be [[interrupted]] during [[debugging]] so that the [[state]] of the program at that point can be [[investigate]]d.
# {{lb|en|meteorology}} A [[location]] [[refer]]red to when [[issue|issuing]] [[watch]]es, [[warning]]s, or [[advisory|advisories]] for [[specific]] [[area]]s.
# {{lb|en|physics}} The point where [[surface water]] [[waves]] are [[break]]ing in, e.g., [[ocean]]s or [[lake]]s.
# {{lb|en|web development}} A [[viewport]] width at which a [[web page]] is programmed to adjust the properties (such as position, size, or visibility) of certain elements within the page.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Charles Wyke-Smith|title=Stylin' with CSS: A Designer's Guide
|passage=If a device's screen width is equal to, or less than, the screen width defined at the '''breakpoint''', it will have the related CSS applied to it. You sometimes want to set breakpoints to match particular device screen widths, but I have found that what is most important is simply that the layout remains usable as it gets smaller.}}

